Make has exactly 1 syntactic rule about tabs: recipe lines — the shell commands under a target — must begin with a hard tab character. Everything else (variable assignments, ifeq/endif, define/endef declarations, comments) has no tab requirement.
The one nuance here is COMPILE_OBJ. The define/endef block's lines don't need their own tabs — what matters is that $(COMPILE_OBJ) is called from a tab-indented recipe position in the pattern rules, which puts Make in recipe context. The expanded content is then treated as recipe commands. So the structure is correct, provided the $(COMPILE_OBJ) call and the link rule commands are tab-indented in the actual source. Since the Makefile produces correct output, they are.
